[#:seon.task{:name "unresolved-sender-logs-unbounded"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Rendering a message whose sender lookup ref does not resolve ([`:seon.agent/id` \"nobody\"]) makes Datahike log an unbounded :error line on every render. AGENTS: a log line printing a value outside the floor is a defect. Resolve the sender as data (a typed unknown) without the logging lookup, or bound the line (fix-html-views 52fb24e1c)."
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/red-triage-3-2026-09-27.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-sender-created-after-the-recipients-branch-cannot-message-it"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`seon.cluster.message/send!` writes the message on the recipient's home branch with `:seon.message/from [:seon.agent/id sender]`; a sender created on the cluster after that branch forked does not exist there, so the writer refuses `:seon.db.write/validation-refusal` \"Nothing found for entity id [:seon.agent/id ...]\" and `my.message/send` answers it. Root and agents older than the branch are unaffected. Decide at `seon.cluster.message/send-call` how a sender absent on the target branch is named (the ref is required there)."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.message/send! seon.cluster.message/delivery my.message/send}}

 #:seon.task{:name "a-test-never-sends-into-the-live-root-inbox"
             :kind :function
             :intent "A test run by an agent on default wrote to the live cluster's root inbox. Found 2026-10-10 on default 6aca2052: root's unread inbox held five \"Hello\" messages from agent resume-after-restart (09:30:28-09:30:38Z, the lane's `my.test/check` runs) plus a test-run error notice at 09:30:48Z (host-test-4733fa33a3d8 branch connections still held). Reproduced as agent chain5-findings: `(my.test/check {:seon.test/changed ['seon.supplied-documentation-test/a-missing-agent-is-refused-by-name-before-the-send ...]})` ran test run e8af284167a3 and message 600bcf5c5e0d (\"Hello\", from chain5-findings, 11:27:01Z) reached root; the test's own claim failed (`select-keys refusal` was {} because the refusal never came: the agent's world supplied `:seon.agent/id`, so `(message/send {:my.message/to \"root\" :my.message/content \"Hello\"})` ran). `seon.cluster.message/send!` resolves the recipient's home branch on the connection it holds and writes there through `seon.db/branch-connection`, so a send whose world is the agent's own branch (not the fixture database the test built) reaches root on the live cluster. AGENTS \"No JVM-global test state\": a test writes only into its own world. No proof is red in the check runner (run of the test there: green), only when an agent runs it (test run e8af284167a3, fail-count 1 on the refusal claim), so the first step is a test that is red in the runner: a fixture cluster whose root lives on another branch than the sender's and a send from the sender's world to root, asserting root's branch holds no new message. Done when the test sends from a world that holds no agent identity as it asserts (the fixture `env/environment` is the only world its body may use) or `send!` refuses a recipient whose home is not a branch of the fixture, and a run of `seon.supplied-documentation-test` by an agent leaves root's inbox unchanged; then root's stale unread messages (five Hello, the 09:30:48Z test notice, agent-error notices from repl-only-3, chain2-forgive-2, message-wakes) are marked received with `my.message/received!`. Root's current-state block lists those unread messages: the same evidence from the context side, covered by this fix and the clearing; nothing separate to file."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.message/send! my.message/send my.message/received! seon.test-support/with-database}
             :evidence #{"docs/seon/plan.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-send-writes-nothing-unless-it-keeps"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Phase P3 of hybrid-agent-world.md (after P7's inbox in-port). Messages are channels: `seon.cluster.message/send!` validates the candidate against the sender's own database (its id minted there), looks the recipient's :seon.agent/inbox channel up in the roster (`seon.cluster.agent/channels`) and `offer!`s the message value, never parking; it answers `seon.cluster.wake/delivery`'s classification: delivered -> {:seon.message/id :seon.message/accepted true}; no channel (not running), closed or full -> the error value :seon.message/undeliverable naming the recipient, its :seon.task/execution intent and the cause, nothing queued (a :running recipient with an open attempt and no graph is hosted by the reconciler's next pass; the sender resends then). The sender writes nothing unless the send declares :my.message/keep? true (new optional member of my.message/message), in which case `send-call` also writes its own copy on its own branch. Deleted: the recipient branch lookup over :seon.agent/branch, `seon.db/branch-connection`, the cross-branch transact and :seon.message/unknown-recipient. Accepted means in the channel, not written; a sender needing proof keeps its copy and waits for a reply; a resend carries the same id and is a no-op at the recipient. Proofs: this test (no transaction on any branch for a plain send; one on the sender's branch with keep?; seon.profile/calls-of of seon.db/branch-connection is zero; send! under 2 ms with no writer wait); a-send-to-an-agent-that-is-not-running-is-answered-as-undeliverable (table: no graph, closed inbox, full inbox -> the error names recipient, intent and cause; nothing queued anywhere). Files: src/seon/cluster/message.clj, src/my/message.clj, resources/seon/schemas/my.message.edn, test/seon/cluster/message_test.clj."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.message/send! seon.cluster.message/send-call my.message/send seon.cluster.agent/channels seon.cluster.wake/delivery seon.profile/calls-of}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/hybrid-agent-world.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "root-offers-a-sent-message-once-and-merges-its-record"
             :kind :function
             :intent "(hybrid-done-and-merge M16, reused U5) The sender only transacts: `seon.cluster.message/send!` writes the message on the sender's own branch with `seon.cluster.message/send-call` (the sender's `offer!` and `:my.message/keep?` go). Root's `seon.cluster.agent/db<->flow` records it on main by `:seon.message/id` before its one `offer!` to the recipient's `:seon.agent/inbox` channel (`seon.cluster.agent/channels`); a crash between leaves 'unknown', never a second offer (in-flight loss accepted); an offer that cannot be made is `:seon.message/undeliverable` (recipient, intent, cause) on main's message entity, never a write to the sender's branch. Red proofs in `seon.cluster.message-test`: the deftest tagged to this task (one offer; main holds the record once by id; a second `db<->flow` run offers nothing) and `an-undeliverable-message-is-answered-as-data` (no graph starts). Cost: under 25 ms per message. Files: `src/seon/cluster/message.clj`, `src/my/message.clj`, `src/seon/cluster/agent.clj`, schemas. After M13 (`seon.turn/a-held-task-opens-no-model-turn`), else a record on main would start a stopped recipient."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.message/send! seon.cluster.message/send-call seon.cluster.agent/mailbox-step seon.cluster.agent/channels seon.cluster.agent/db<->flow seon.cluster.wake/delivery}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/hybrid-done-and-merge.md"}}]
